Q: Javascript: the code after `yield` is never executed A: <p>The transition in <code>authenticate()</code> is causing your component to be destroyed before the rest of the code can execute. Refactor so that the <code>authenticate</code> method doesn't handle the transition but just returns (resolves to) a status about the authentication. Your task then takes the appropriate action from there. As this is a component, transitions should be handled in the upstream <em>route</em> or <em>controller</em> and the component should just call the upstream action it has been passed.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>signinTask: task(function* () {
  ...
  try {
    const authenticated = yield this.get('session').authenticate('authenticator:api', {
      identification: email,
      password,
    });

    if (authenticated) {
        this.get('metrics').trackEvent({ 
          email,                         
          event: 'Log in',               
        });
    }

    // Call upstream action
    if (this.onSignIn) this.onSignIn();
  } catch (error) {
    ...
  }
}).restartable(),
</code></pre>
<p>In recent versions of Ember/Ember-concurrency you can use the <code>async/await</code> pattern which you might find easier than the generator  <code>task*/yield</code> pattern</p>
<pre><code>signinTask = task({ restartable: true}, async () =&gt; {
  ...
  try {
    const authenticated = await this.get('session').authenticate('authenticator:api', {
      identification: email,
      password,
    });

    if (authenticated) {
        this.get('metrics').trackEvent({ 
          email,                         
          event: 'Log in',               
        });
    }

    // Call upstream action using Octane method
    if (this.args.onSignIn) this.args.onSignIn();
  } catch (error) {
    ...
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Embedded static build in iframe (Electron Forge + Vite) A: <p>To the best of my knowledge, the issues you're seeing are symptoms of using the file:// protocol. In Electorn, file:// has no concept of a &quot;Project root&quot;: it treates / as the root of the entire hard drive.</p>
<p><strong>The Solution: Custom Privileged Protocols</strong></p>
<p>Instead of fighting Vite or refactoring a tear-inducing number of lines, you should create a Custom Protocl (e.g., app:// or webapp://). This creates a virtual &quot;Origin&quot; that mimics a real web server.</p>
<p><strong>First: Register the Protocol (Main Process)</strong></p>
<p>In your main.ts, you need to register the scheme as privileged (so it supports Fetch/CORS) and handle the requests. Use <code>protocol.handle</code> and <code>net.fetch</code> for modern, stream-based file serving.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { app, protocol, net } from 'electron';
import path from 'path';
import { patToFileURL } from 'url';

// 1. MUST be called before app is ready
protocol.registerSchemesAsPriveleged([
  { scheme: 'webapp', privileges: { standard: true, secure: true, supportFetchAPI:  true} }
]);

app.whenReady().then(() =&gt; {
    protocl.handle('webapp', (request) =&gt;
        let urlPath = new URL(reqest.url).pathname;
        urlPath = decodeURIComponent(urlPath);

        // Solve Problem #1: Directory Indexx Fallback
        if (urlPath.endsWith('/') || urlPath == '') {
            urlPath += 'index.html';
        }

        //Solve Problem #2: Environment-aware path resolution
        const resourcePath = app.isPackaged
            ? path.join(process.resourcesPath, 'static_build', urlPath)
            : path.join(__dirname, '../../public/static_build', urlPath);

        // Use net.fetch + pathToFileURL to handle MIME types and Windows paths automaticly
    });
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Then, Configure Asset Packaging</strong></p>
<p>To ensure your <code>static_build</code> is actually there in prod, add it to <code>extraResource</code> in your <code>forge.config.ts</code> . This keeps  it outside the <code>.asar</code> for easier access or inside if your prefer, but <code>process.resourcesPath</code> will always find it.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// forge.config.ts
packagerConfig: {
    extraResource: [
        path.resolve(__dirname, 'ppublic/static_build')
    ]
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Usage in Renderer</strong></p>
<p>Now your iframe source is stable across all envs. Absolute paths like <code>&lt;img src=&quot;/logo.png&quot;&gt;</code> inside the iframe will resolve correctly to <code>webapp://local/logo.png</code> .</p>
<p><strong>Lastly, Embedded App Routing</strong></p>
<p>Since the custom protocol provides a consistent root, set your React Router basename to / and your Vite base to /.</p>
<p>(I apologize for any typos in advance)</p>
